ate (I, 9), it\'s empty, and it will give me an immediate defensive advantage. G9 is a trap and other moves allow an easy Black 3. The logic is solid.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The game is in the early opening phase. Black has established a diagonal connection with stones at G7 and H8, threatening to extend this into a 3-in-a-row (Open 3) by playing at I9 or F6. White has a single stone at H9. The most critical task for White is to block Black\'s diagonal momentum while simultaneously developing its own line.\n- **I9**: This is the most promising move. It directly blocks Black from extending the G7-H8 diagonal to I9. Furthermore, it places a stone adjacent to White\'s existing stone at H9, forming a horizontal 2-in-a-row (H9-I9), creating a base for future attacks.\n- **F6**: This move blocks the other end of Black\'s diagonal. However, it is far from White\'s stone at H9, leaving White\'s pieces disconnected and allowing Black to dictate the flow in the center (e.g., Black could play I9 later or surround H9).\n- **G9**: This forms a horizontal 2 with H9 but fails to address Black\'s primary threat. Black would likely respond with I9, creating a strong 3-in-a-row diagonal, putting White on the defensive.\n\nReason: White must block Black\'s potential 3-in-a-row diagonal extension. Moving to I9 achieves this defense while also connecting with the existing White stone at H9 to form an offensive shape (horizontal 2). This move balances defense and development effectively.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "I", "row": 9}'}
